/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - At collapse the top fibre is at the crushing strain εcu = 0.0035.
   - εs is the strain at the tension steel.
   - IS 456 calls the steel yielded when εs reaches εs,lim = 0.87 fy/Es + 0.002, with Es = 2 × 10⁵ N/mm².
   - For Fe250: 0.0035/(0.0055 + 0.00109) = 0.531; Fe415: 0.0035/(0.0055 + 0.00181) = 0.479; Fe500: 0.0035/(0.0055 + 0.00218) = 0.456.
   - Some texts call these tension-controlled and compression-controlled sections; the test is still xu against xu,max, or Ast against Ast,lim.
   - At the balanced point C = T with the steel at 0.87 fy:
   - Ast,lim is the most tension steel a singly reinforced section may have, in mm²; pt,lim the same as a percentage of b d; 41.4 = 100 × 0.36/0.87.
   - A higher steel grade allows less steel; a stronger concrete allows more.
   - If 0.87 fy Ast/(0.36 fck b) gives xu above xu,max, that xu is wrong: it assumed the steel was at 0.87 fy, but it has not yielded.
   - If more moment is needed, compression steel (doubly reinforced beams) raises the capacity and keeps the beam ductile.
*/
