/* book exam traps: Calling a beam under-reinforced because its steel looks small. 1500 mm² is under-reinforced in the 300 × 500 M30 beam above but would be over-reinforced in a 230 × 300 M20 beam (Ast,lim = 0.36 × 20 × 230 × 0.48 × 250/361.05 = 550 mm² for d = 250 mm). | Using xu,max/d = 0.48 for Fe500. The value is 0.46; with 0.48 the limiting moment of the example would be 310.4 kN·m instead of 300.6 kN·m. | Using 0.36 fck b xu = 0.87 fy Ast for an over-reinforced section. In example (b) it gives 335.6 mm against the true 302.8 mm, because the steel stress is only 392.4 N/mm², not 435 N/mm². | Taking the yield strain fy/Es as the balanced strain. IS 456 uses 0.87 fy/Es + 0.002; with fy/Es alone, xu,max/d for Fe415 would come out 0.63. | Comparing pt with a fixed value such as 1 % instead of pt,lim for the actual concrete and steel grades. */
